  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery early this afternoon shows broad cyclonic flow \nover northern and western portions of the CONUS, with one shortwave \nimpacting the Upper Midwest and another in the Pacific Northwest. \nThis leaves southwest flow aloft into the local area with a narrow \nstream of moisture from the central Rockies into the High Plains. At \nthe surface, low pressure extends from western KS northeastward to \nnear the SD/MN border, in association with the first of the \naforementioned shortwaves. The better upper support for showers and \nstorms will be well to our north into IA/MN, but some isolated or \nscattered activity may manage to develop in our area with \nconvergence along the front as it passes this evening into the \novernight hours. Other limiting factors for coverage are the waning \ninstability and lapse rates by the time the front makes it into \nnorth central KS. Due to this, it is uncertain how long any rain may \npersist as the boundary moves southeast overnight, but have \nmaintained some low chance PoPs until around 4-5am.\n\nThe front should settle in the area Thursday afternoon, though there \nis some uncertainty as to where exactly it will set up. Short-term \nguidance seems to be trending southward, near or perhaps just south \nof I-70. These models are suggesting temperatures may struggle to \nget above 70 degrees north of the boundary, whereas highs south of \nit are forecast to be in the upper 70s to near 80. Similar to \ntonight, CAMs are suggesting isolated showers or storms to develop \nnear the front during the early evening, but coverage looks limited \nwith modest lapse rates around 6.5 C/km.\n\nFriday brings the next best chance of rain as the southern stream \ntrough lifts northeast into the area. Some isentropic lift ahead of \nthe wave could start showers overnight Thursday night into early \nFriday, but chances peak during the afternoon and evening when \nvorticity advection increases. Again, lapse rates don't look much \nbetter than the next couple of days, so while some pockets of \nlightning and thunder are possible, it doesn't appear probable for a \ngiven location. This wave slowly pushes east into Saturday, leaving \nconditions to become drier for evening Halloween activities.\n\nSunday brings a break from rain chances while the next more potent \ntrough deepens over the western US and moves into the area Monday. \nThis stronger system looks to bring the most rain to the area during \nthe next week in terms of amounts and probably coverage as well. \nThere is still enough variability in the guidance in how this trough \nevolves Monday into Tuesday, leading to uncertainty in timing and \nlocation of associated surface features. The cold front should move \nthrough the area by Tuesday. As such, temperatures look to remain \nabove average through the end of this week with highs in the 70s, \nbut should get closer to mid 60s behind the front. Clouds and rain \ncould still result in temperatures on the cooler side of guidance \nat times, particularly on Saturday.",
